WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.831
Speaker 1: Well, if you've not been paying
attention to the news for the last 20 or so

00:00:04.831 --> 00:00:12.950
hours closer to 24 now, Vladimir Putin's Russia
has started an invasion of Ukraine.

00:00:12.950 --> 00:00:19.430
And this is I mean, listen, you know, at a
certain point when you see 190000 troops massing

00:00:19.430 --> 00:00:26.250
at the border and you understand that it really
is starting to not make sense merely as a

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:29.450
posturing exercise what Putin is doing.

00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:35.780
You start to consider this might be the precursor
to some kind of an invasion and almost exactly

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:43.140
as predicted by many Vladimir Putin creating
a pretext for that invasion, using all sorts

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:50.010
of fluffy language like peacekeeping to justify
that invasion, doing it slowly.

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:53.910
But a slow invasion is still an invasion,
and that's what we have going on.

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:59.810
Stock markets around the world very rattled
in anticipation of what may be very erratic

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:05.779
energy prices, further supply chain delays
and all of the economic cycle.

00:01:05.779 --> 00:01:10.380
We will talk about once we get a sense of
what the scope of this is going to be.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:16.929
But for the time being, sort of following
the Georgia playbook, that's not Atlanta,

00:01:16.929 --> 00:01:18.889
Georgia, where Marjorie Taylor Greene is from.

00:01:18.889 --> 00:01:27.090
I'm talking about the country of Georgia and
possibly all of this meant to goad Ukraine

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:33.929
into an overreaction to provide an even bigger
pretext for a larger scale conflict.

00:01:33.929 --> 00:01:37.529
So let's kind of talk through what is going
on yesterday.

00:01:37.529 --> 00:01:40.399
OK, so let me back up.

00:01:40.399 --> 00:01:48.619
Even there are what we might call separatists
in Ukraine separatist, meaning they don't

00:01:48.619 --> 00:01:50.770
want to be part of Ukraine.

00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:53.249
They want to be part of Russia, OK?

00:01:53.249 --> 00:01:59.059
Two of the regions in which there are such
separatists are regions, and I'm going to

00:01:59.059 --> 00:02:01.319
do my best with the pronunciation here.

00:02:01.319 --> 00:02:08.099
The Donetsk region and the Luhansk region,
these are on east eastern Ukraine.

00:02:08.099 --> 00:02:12.569
You can pull it up on Google Maps and sort
of take a look at what where these are.

00:02:12.569 --> 00:02:18.220
So what Vladimir Putin did early yesterday
or actually it was early in the U.S. it was

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:27.230
already kind of in the afternoon in Russia
and Ukraine is recognize those as regions

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:33.439
as as legitimize legitimately recognize those
areas as independent.

00:02:33.439 --> 00:02:36.879
So Putin is saying, OK, it's part of Ukraine.

00:02:36.879 --> 00:02:38.769
Donetsk is part of Ukraine.

00:02:38.769 --> 00:02:40.019
Luhansk is part of Ukraine.

00:02:40.019 --> 00:02:44.920
But there are separatists there who quote,
really are Russian.

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:46.959
They speak the language.

00:02:46.959 --> 00:02:48.629
They want to be part of Russia.

00:02:48.629 --> 00:02:52.680
So we're going to say those are independent
regions, not Ukrainian regions.

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:55.329
Now if you Ukraine, you say, Wait, hold on.

00:02:55.329 --> 00:02:56.500
It's it's part of the country.

00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:57.500
It's Ukraine.

00:02:57.500 --> 00:02:58.969
But Putin says, no, those are.

00:02:58.969 --> 00:03:01.390
Those are independent regions.

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:08.129
He then hours later, after some mild sanctions
were imposed by the EU and the United States

00:03:08.129 --> 00:03:10.629
on on Putin for doing this.

00:03:10.629 --> 00:03:16.340
Hours later, and really, the sanctions were
imposed on those regions rather than on what

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:18.480
we might consider Russia proper.

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:25.109
Putin says, Hey, the separatists in those
quote independent areas that have recognized

00:03:25.109 --> 00:03:27.550
they are going, they are under attack.

00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:32.480
So we need to go in and send in peacekeeping
forces into that area.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:37.860
It's the Putin playbook hook, line and sinker,
and there shouldn't really be anything surprising

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:39.129
about it.

00:03:39.129 --> 00:03:41.569
This is what he was signaling for a long time.

00:03:41.569 --> 00:03:45.310
This is what various foreign policy analysts
and experts were saying could could be done

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:49.579
for a long time, some kind of pretext to then
send in troops.

00:03:49.579 --> 00:03:51.840
But you know, they call them peacekeeping.

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:54.060
But it doesn't matter how slow it is.

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:56.310
It doesn't matter if you say you're there
to keep the peace.

00:03:56.310 --> 00:04:00.159
It is still fundamentally an invasion.

00:04:00.159 --> 00:04:07.060
We have video where you see tanks rolling
down the road.

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:11.139
This was yesterday, shortly after this announcement
was made.

00:04:11.139 --> 00:04:16.400
And of course, the question now is to what
degree is this going to escalate or grow now?

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:20.299
There are some people who will talk about
it a little bit who are really going out of

00:04:20.299 --> 00:04:24.300
their way to sort of defend and normalize
what Russia is doing.

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:29.210
And you can be against American involvement
and be against war and be against violence

00:04:29.210 --> 00:04:35.729
the way that I am and still recognize that
this is a classic Russian playbook, that this

00:04:35.729 --> 00:04:42.220
is an invasion, that Russia has no right to
send tanks where they are sending tanks into

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:44.550
the Donetsk and Luhansk regions.

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:48.540
And just imagine a similar situation with
Mexico or Quebec.

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:54.660
These are not perfect analogies, but imagine
that Mexico said, Hey, you know what?

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:58.280
There are people in Texas and Arizona they
look like.

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:06.710
US and they speak Spanish, our language, and
they would prefer to be part of Mexico rather

00:05:06.710 --> 00:05:14.229
than the United States in Texas or Arizona,
so we are recognizing these enclaves of Mexican-Americans

00:05:14.229 --> 00:05:15.410
as Mexico.

00:05:15.410 --> 00:05:18.130
They are independent as far as Mexico is concerned.

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:22.840
Well, in the U.S., we would say, yeah, but
that's the United States.

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:24.830
And I mean, they could go to Mexico.

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:25.830
You're not stop.

00:05:25.830 --> 00:05:27.460
If you want to welcome them, they could go.

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:32.050
And then Mexico says, Well, listen, on the
basis of the independence of these separatists,

00:05:32.050 --> 00:05:36.389
we're going to send some peacekeeping troops
and come on, guys, nobody would go for it.

00:05:36.389 --> 00:05:38.020
Think of Quebec.

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:43.139
Another analogy These are not perfect analogies,
but they are useful in thinking about this.

00:05:43.139 --> 00:05:45.910
In Quebec, there is a separatist movement.

00:05:45.910 --> 00:05:49.770
Imagine that that separatist movement in Quebec,
Canada, said, Well, we don't want to be our

00:05:49.770 --> 00:05:51.099
own country.

00:05:51.099 --> 00:05:53.250
We want to be part of the United States.

00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:59.200
Would the U.S. have any right to say, Hey,
those Quebec separatists, we think of them

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:01.870
as independent because they really want to
be part of us.

00:06:01.870 --> 00:06:04.210
Oh, they need us to help keep them safe.

00:06:04.210 --> 00:06:05.770
We're going to send some tanks there.

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:06.770
Give me a break, guys.

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:08.440
It's absolutely absurd.

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:15.710
Now we have video from the EU foreign policy
chief Josep Borrell, who is still kind of

00:06:15.710 --> 00:06:18.610
hedging a little bit as to the terms that
are being used.

00:06:18.610 --> 00:06:20.319
Let's take a listen.

00:06:20.319 --> 00:06:28.379
Speaker 2: Well, Russian troops has entered
in Donbass, we consider Donbas part of Ukraine.

00:06:28.379 --> 00:06:33.990
So certainly the Russian troops enter it into
the dramatic Ukrainian.

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:39.800
And we're going to say that a fully fledged
invasion by Russian troops on the Ukrainian

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:40.800
soldiers.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:41.800
Speaker 1: Yeah, they've invaded.

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:43.460
One part of Russia is what we.

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:46.170
I'm sorry, one part of Ukraine.

00:06:46.170 --> 00:06:50.659
The idea may be just to do this and then back
off.

00:06:50.659 --> 00:06:51.659
We don't know.

00:06:51.659 --> 00:06:53.509
And again, I hope that people recognize that
all along.

00:06:53.509 --> 00:06:58.280
I've said we can make assessments based on
the signals, but we don't really know what

00:06:58.280 --> 00:06:59.819
Putin plans to do.

00:06:59.819 --> 00:07:04.030
This could just be one tiny step a day, so
there's never any one day where it's like,

00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:09.030
Wow, look at what happened, and then they
move towards Kiev.

00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:13.080
It's just it's not completely clear.

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:21.189
We are also going to have to consider what
Putin's greater ideology here is.

00:07:21.189 --> 00:07:27.500
And it's sort of like the idea of pointing
back to the former USSR.

00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:32.540
Ukraine is really important in Putin's mind
in kind of recreating that, and we'll talk

00:07:32.540 --> 00:07:36.280
about that on the bonus show today, but also
in terms of tactics.

00:07:36.280 --> 00:07:42.069
This is really kind of back to like an old
war concept, which is can you just change

00:07:42.069 --> 00:07:47.159
your country's borders by force since World
War Two?

00:07:47.159 --> 00:07:53.750
And since the codification in international
law that prohibits the threat or use of force

00:07:53.750 --> 00:07:59.419
against territorial integrity or political
independence, we've seen few attempts to just

00:07:59.419 --> 00:08:02.310
change your country's border by force in this
degree.

00:08:02.310 --> 00:08:07.819
Now you could say, Well, you know, there's
a bunch of areas of the border in India where

00:08:07.819 --> 00:08:14.080
there are constant disputes and small skirmishes
and little bits of land taken and given back,

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:17.530
and it is changing the border in a way by
force.

00:08:17.530 --> 00:08:23.310
In major ways, there have really only been
four attempts since World War Two North Korea

00:08:23.310 --> 00:08:31.200
in 1950 trying to conquer South Korea, a North
Vietnam successfully conquering South Vietnam

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:38.150
in 75, Indonesia annexing Timor Leste in 75.

00:08:38.150 --> 00:08:44.970
And that very short, very short lived Iraqi
annexation of Kuwait in 1990, before it was

00:08:44.970 --> 00:08:47.330
liberated in what we call the first Gulf War.

00:08:47.330 --> 00:08:51.340
So this is not super common since World War
Two.

00:08:51.340 --> 00:08:56.740
It will be important not only to watch what
happens in Russia, but also to watch China's

00:08:56.740 --> 00:09:01.260
behavior and ambitions on the basis of what
happens with Russia.

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:02.260
Ukraine.

00:09:02.260 --> 00:09:07.950
Because if Russia gets away with this, China's
interest in similar action with Taiwan may

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:08.950
increase.

00:09:08.950 --> 00:09:14.480
So a lot at play here and in the immediate,
it's going to remain extraordinarily chaotic

